pen to be able to have the  opportunity to do honors projects with  students having them create mathematical  demonstrations and working through and  having them learn how to do that is  actually a really good way to  incorporate Mathematica one on one but  obviously not all of our classes are  one-on-one we're not all tutoring when  we're teaching and so over the years  I've had the opportunity any opportunity  to teach 30 to 60 size student classes  and these sizes are actually most  comparable to the old calculus and  mathematica course sizes and so this one  is on the some level the most comparable  to what had happened in the calculus  Mathematica program so I did this with  calc 1 2 and differential equations and  depending on the class a course number  and course credit I would meet with the  students one to two days in a  traditional lecture format and then one  to two days in the computer lab and in  contrast to other institutions that have  attempted to incorporate you know  Mathematica or any of the competitors a  key factor was as the instructor I also  ran the labs so there was a very fluid  transition that you would not  be seeing in if you read about the  papers of other people who are trying to  incorporate technology in their classes  right now so what did I do um primarily  I did what I had been asked to do and  that was basically to capitalize on the  improvements to both Mathematica in the  last 25 years and in our users the  students familiarity with computers in  the last 25 years we can all probably  admit that there are students who are  much more comfortable with technology  out there than we are as the instructors  and so um at the request of the  department I wrote new assignments that  both you know took advantage of  mathematical improvements and students  improvements when it comes to being able  to use technology and so these new  assignments taught students how to use  Mathematica commands for themselves that  was really the primary goal of these new  assignments and as we see it was fairly  successful in some classes and how did  we do that how did we teach students to  basically take command of Mathematica  further own self first we gradually  introduce students to the commands in  the assignments and the commands  introduced an assignment were directly  relevant to the concepts for that lesson  so we aren't going to introduce the  integrate command when we're talking  about derivatives and you know we  introduced the limit command at the  beginning of the semester when it was  relevant so they were directly relevant  to the concepts of that lesson but we  didn't just throw it to them we built  from the basics and we first said here's  the anatomy of the command and here's a  quick example so if you want to compute  a limit here's what it will look like in  a generic sense and here's an example  and so we pop them through short  practical examples but in the homework  assignments that the students had to  work through they weren't just short  practical problems they were often long  and involved thought processes that had  to be going into some of the problems  and so we said when you don't know how  to do something with your command refer  to the documentation center so we  empowered them by turning to the  greatest resource out there is to  actually learn from learn for themselves  how  to continue to expand their  understanding of the commands in  Mathematica and all of their options  another feature that I included with  these new home works were that I wrote  problem kind of descriptions so I told  the students you know what commands they  could expect to use now some of them  were very creative and would include  commands that I didn't teach them but  they went into the Documentation Center  and found on their own you know we also  included what the students should learn  from answering the question and why we  actually had that problem in the overall  homework for pretty much every single  homework question and very similar to  how the calculus Mathematica courses had  been run we had homework submitted by  groups of two to four students and then  they were personally graded and returned  with feedback to the students but the  primary change here was that we've  really capitalized on the advances the  last 25 years and really updated the  material and made it a little bit more  modular which we'll get into later on as  the primary benefit so just some brief  examples of how we did this I went down  and I just tried to type from memory a  you know a sampling of the commands that  students learned this is in calc 1 over  the course of last fall and last spring  so those are some of the commands that  the students learned and there are  certainly ones that you might expect to  see so the plot command and some of the  options and then one you know the prime  pie that came up in a random problem but  it was still one that the students got  to use it was kind of fun I figured I'd  just put up here two or three  visualizations from calc two so of  course that I taught last spring here  we're looking at visualizing and  computing terms of a convergent sequence  so often we talk about the word  convergence and divergence and in very  abstract senses students don't get it  but in a very concrete picture students  can look at that and understand that  sequence is converging in this case to  the value of two and at least gut  reaction understand what we mean when  we're throwing around these mathematical  terms this is a personal favorite that  students liked it is a visualization of  the alternating series test or rather  just looking at an alternate  series so in blue you have the terms of  the series and then oh the orange color  blue and orange colors of the U of I the  orange represents the partial sums so  students can see that the changes in  partial sums corresponding to the actual  terms of the series and they could  gather this information about why the  are alternating series test in this case  worked and what it really meant for the  series to be converging and of course  calc 2 is not complete without a  visualization of our favorite Gabriel's  horn you know the the object that has  infinite volume but finite surface area  students are always amazed when they say  you mean I can I can fill it sorry  finite volume but infinite surface area  you can fill it with paint but you  cannot paint the surface area of this  object now what are some conclusions  from updating and creating new homework  assignments that students it in groups  the first conclusion is that students  actually had far greater of a personal  investment when I use these new homework  assignments then to the previous course  materials that I had at the U of I  because they realized that they were  able to solve problems on their own in  Mathematica we taught them how to use  the commands and by the end of the  semester I can tell you that absolutely  every single student could use the plot  command without even thinking about it  and probably many more but the idea is  when they realized that they were able  to personally solve their problems they  have a lot more of a personal investment  rather than just taking you know a page  of code they were able to put in a  personal interest into solving their  homework questions the second conclusion  is an interesting one but I think it's  similar to a lot of the other studies  that are being done right now these  homeworks actually help to differentiate  for me the diligent eager to learn  students from those who were less  motivated so I'm comparing calculus one  I taught it last fall and then I taught  it again in the spring and when I taught  in the fall the average course grade was  eighty-four percent the students in the  fall were very diligent they were  hardworking when they encountered gaps  in their previous learning they put in  the  for to improve on their mistakes and to  fill in those gaps from anything they  might have missed in high school the  students in the spring however one let's  point out some differences I was able to  revise a lot of the material from the  fall that fed into the spring material  so they should have had you know better  material I was also meeting with them  one more day a week than I had been in  the fall but at the end of the day their  course average was a sixty-nine percent  these students on average came in a lot  weaker and many of them did not put  forth that personal effort necessary to  improve over the semester all of the  students in the spring had seen calculus  one before all of them had failed  calculus one before some of them would  just skip midterms they didn't really  care too much to put forth that effort  to do well so what was the impact on  their end of the semester assessments  from the fall the students who worked  hard fifty percent of the students on  their course evaluations said that  Mathematica when they're asked what was  the most beneficial aspect of the course  fifty percent listed Mathematica in  these assignments that we did and you  know some of the quotes you can see  right there mathematica allowed me to  visualize many parts of math I didn't  understand before so for these  hard-working students fifty percent of  them listed Mathematica the most  beneficial aspect compare this to the  spring 21.4 percent said that  Mathematica was beneficial but 28.6  percent of the students said that  Mathematica was the one aspect of the  course that they would change so this  goes to show that for the students who  really want to are motivated and want to  learn mathematica especially the  visualization and being able to see  things um and have that quick  computation it was valued and  appreciated and it helped them for the  students however who didn't want to  personally push themselves it was not  viewed as favorably and it did not help  them as much so this was useful it was  beneficial but if you're a student at  the University of Illinois and a ver  student you don't get to go through a  class of 30 to 60 students the average  student at the University of Illinois  when they take calculus will go through  large lecture sections of calculus that  are going to be 200 to 250 students per  lecture section so the question that the  department wanted to ask this year is  can we still use Mathematica in the  large lectures and that question I  answered with yes to some extent we can  still use some aspects of Mathematica  with the large lecture classes so what  are we doing this year at the U of I  this fall we're working with two large  lecture sections of calc 1 and then in  the spring this upcoming spring we'll be  following up with two large lecture  sections of calculus two and these large  lecture sections I said 200 250 students  they'll meet two days a week with a  faculty member in the classroom and then  they'll break down into small ta lead  discussion sections 40 to 50 students  the other two days of the week and each  section when they meet with the graduate  TA and they in keeping with the  tradition of having the peer advisors we  kept to undergraduate assistance to go  into each of those discussion sections  and help the graduate student out but in  doing something on such a massive scale  we had to think and answer a lot of  questions and say what aspects of  mathematica can you scale up if you're  going to be doing a course of the old  this size and the conclusion we came to  is that you could not personally grade  group home works anymore it is not  feasible to do it on this scale but we  didn't want to lose what we saw from the  smaller classes that visual benefit and  the computational benefit we wanted to  keep that and so that's what we said  well let's go ahead and develop a bunch  of CDF demonstrations and the way we're  going to incorporate it is sometimes in  the classroom I definitely bring it into  the classroom but when they're doing  their active discussion worksheets with  the Graduate ta two days a week in their  small  we're going to have some of those  questions the ones that require them to  access a CDF and so what we've done is  normally the students would go to their  ta section and they would work in groups  of two to four on paper worksheets and  it was it's very the University of  Illinois is very excited to be doing a  lot of active discussion learning in  these sections and so we're keeping that  energy in these discussion sections but  now the paper worksheets about half of  the questions this semester are  directing students to CDF demos that  I've created and some of them that you  know it's helpful to visualize things  and other times they need it for  computation so one thing to note though  is that in using these CDF's students  are not going to be using Mathematica  commands or code anymore that is one  thing we had to let go of but the good  news is that these CDF's are providing  students with that prompt prompt  computation and concept visualization  which is certainly an improvement over  static textbook figures we can all agree  that the manipulate command is certainly  something that we can't live without now  at least I cannot live without in the  classroom so here are two examples i'm  going to show you a bad example and a  good example of how you can use a CDF in  class so first the bad example when  you're dealing with related rates you  sometimes get the angle of repose  question of if you're pouring corn out  onto the floor of a barn you know what's  the shape that it you know that it makes  and so we have this manipulate that kind  of shows the corn pile growing um it's a  good graphic but really it didn't do  anything for students it doesn't know  none of the students said they liked it  in fact some of them said they would  have just rather had you know a  two-dimensional sketch on their paper so  there really isn't any point to  including this as a demonstration what  I'm about to show you though is  something that students only halfway  through the semester have already said  is one of the best things they've seen  in this course and that is a  visualization of the mean value theorem  so I'm going to shrink the size  just a little bit so that we can see  this the entire thing but the mean value  theorem when you first present it in the  classroom students eyes glaze over and  that is because it's a bunch of bath  jargon and if they don't stop and think  about it they're really not going to  understand what you're talking about  it's just a bunch of symbols on the  paper so what we wanted to do was have a  graphical representation of the mean  value theorem and we told students  before they went to do that worksheet  that we want you to use the graphical to  make sense of the mathematical and other  times we'll give them a graph and say  use the math to explain what you see in  the graph so the mean value theorem says  that you know for any two points that  you pick on a continuous function  there's some point between them that has  the same instantaneous rate of change or  the slope of its tangent line is equal  to the slope of the secant line between  the other two points and so students  were able to play around pick any two  points a and B they wanted or even have  manipulate basically do an animation of  what's going on and students would sit  there for a few minutes but they all  came back the day after they did that  worksheet in one of the sections and  they said this helped they now  understand what the mean value theorem  is talking about because they had this  concrete visualization they were able to  use the graphical to make sense of the  mathematical and so this is what I would  say is a useful implementation of the  CDF in the classroom or in the  discussion sections the students got to  experience what it really means for the  mean value theorem visually to make  sense of what we say when we're out the  board so even though we're only halfway  through the semester I do have a few  conclusions based on the data we've been  collecting in the first conclusion not  even based on data all of these CDF's  maybe module ii incorporated into any  course i substitute taught for someone  yesterday who incorporates absolutely no  technology into his calc 1 section and I  still took a CDF in there of Riemann  sums and that's because students don't  really need to have  previous experience they just need to be  someone who's comfortable using a  computer screen and pretty much all of  our students are at this point um we did  have to trade students learning how to  personally use mathematica on this scale  but in exchange we're still having  students harness the power of  Mathematica without that steep learning  curve we're still getting all the  benefits and in fact we're extending the  benefits too in order of magnitude more  students than had previously been  reached the last conclusion though is  one that every teacher faces while these  concepts are beautifully demonstrated in  CDF's the CDF's themselves are unable to  for students to apply themselves and  this is again a similar concept to what  we saw in the conclusion from those 30  to 60 student classes you cannot force  students to apply themselves and this  can be problem ental for students are  detrimental for students on exams no  matter what size the class is but we are  collecting a lot of data on how this is  going this semester so probably in  another year we'll have more concrete  numbers to give for it but one thing I  wanted to conclude with is I don't think  that 400 or 500 is really an upper bound  I know it's a rather large class we're  going to have a large sample size to  work with but I don't think there's  actually an upper bound how many  students you can reach online classes  are here to stay there's not much  question about that the question is how  do we utilize them how are we what are  we 